course: everyday
lesson: 89
title: Handling anger
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Anger is a normal feeling, but it makes the body ready to fight: the heart beats fast, the face goes hot and the fists clench, and the thinking part of the brain catches up only after a few seconds.
explain: That is why stopping, taking slow deep breaths and counting to ten before you speak or act works: it gives your thinking brain time to catch up.
explain: Anger needs a safe way out: walking away, squeezing a pillow, stamping outside, or talking to an adult; it must never come out by hitting, breaking things or saying cruel words.
explain: A boy in Vancouver notices his chest getting tight when his sister takes his Lego; he stops, breathes slowly, counts to ten, and then asks his mum to help sort it out.
fact: Anger makes the body ready to fight, and the thinking part of the brain catches up only after a few seconds.
fact: Stopping, breathing slowly and counting to ten before you speak or act helps you calm down.
fact: Never hit; walk away, squeeze a pillow, stamp your feet outside or tell an adult instead.
fact: Angry words can hurt for a long time, and saying sorry after you calm down repairs the trust.
fact: Slamming doors can trap fingers, break things and frighten others, so close doors gently and use words when calm.
fact: When a game keeps beating you, a break and a drink of water help, because a tired, angry brain plays worse.
fact: People listen to a calm, clear reason much more than to a shout.
fact: Early warning signs like a tight chest or a hot face are a signal to pause, and anger is easier to calm the earlier you catch it.
fact: If you broke something in anger, tell an adult and think how to make it right, because owning up keeps people's trust.
fact: Revenge usually makes a fight bigger, so tell a teacher instead of planning payback.
fact: Holding on to anger for days keeps you unhappy, so talk it through and try to forgive.
exercise: Your brother takes your things and your face goes hot and your fists clench. What do you do first? | Stop, breathe and count to ten | Grab all your things back from him at once | Shout at him as loud as you possibly can | Hit him hard so that he learns not to do it again
exercise: Why does counting to ten help when you are angry? | It gives your thinking brain time to catch up | It makes the other person go away and leave you alone | It uses up all the anger at once | It makes you forget what happened
exercise: You are so furious you want to hit someone. What should you do instead? | Walk away and squeeze a pillow or tell an adult | Hit something they own instead | Hit them only softly, so that it does not hurt them much | Push them so it is not a hit
exercise: You shouted a mean word at your mum. What should you do? | Calm down, then come back and say sorry | Pretend it never happened | Say it again so she knows you really meant it | Stay in your room for the whole week
exercise: You are in a rage and about to slam your door. What is better? | Close it gently and talk once you are calm | Slam it as hard as you can | Kick it instead of slamming it | Lock yourself in and refuse to come out until everyone says sorry
exercise: A video game keeps beating you and you are getting angry. What helps? | Take a break, have water and come back later | Throw the controller across the room | Play all night without stopping until you finally win | Shout at the screen to let it out
exercise: Something really is unfair. How should you say so? | Calmly, saying what is unfair and what you would like | By shouting louder and louder until someone listens to you | By sulking and refusing to talk | By breaking a rule to get even
exercise: Which is an early warning sign of anger? | A tight chest and a hot face | Yawning and feeling very sleepy | Feeling hungry just before lunch | Wanting to laugh at a funny joke
exercise: You broke your toy on purpose because you were furious. What now? | Tell an adult and think how to make it right | Hide all the pieces under the bed where no one looks | Blame your little sister | Ask for a new toy at once
exercise: A classmate tripped you, and you want revenge. What should you do? | Tell a teacher instead of planning payback | Trip him back at lunchtime | Get all your friends to gang up on him after school | Wait and break his pencil case
exercise: You are still angry at a friend after three days. What helps? | Talk it through and try to forgive | Stay angry until they say sorry first | Tell everyone in class what they did | Find a new friend and ignore them for good
exercise: Why do people listen better to a calm reason than to a shout? | A calm voice helps them hear what you mean | Shouting is too quiet to hear | Calm people are always right about everything | People are scared of quiet voices
exercise: Which is true about anger? | Feeling angry is normal, but hurting others is not | Angry feelings are always wrong | Strong people never get angry about anything at all | You must always hide your anger
test: A girl in Liverpool feels her heart pounding when her friend cheats in a game. What is the wisest first step? | Pause and breathe before she says anything | Tell the friend she hates her | Throw the whole game board onto the floor in front of her | Cheat as well to make it fair
test: Why might you say something you regret when you are very angry? | The thinking part of the brain has not caught up yet | Anger always makes you more honest than you usually are | Angry words are always true | Your mouth works faster when you are hot
test: A boy kicked the wall in a temper and hurt his foot. Which would have been a safer way to let anger out? | Stamping his feet outside or squeezing a pillow | Kicking the door instead of the wall | Throwing his shoes at the window | Punching a friend's school bag as hard as he could instead
test: Two boys argue; one plans to scratch the other's bike after school. What will most likely happen? | The fight will get bigger and both will be in trouble | The other boy will say sorry at once | Everything will be fair and forgotten | The teacher will thank him for sorting it out himself
test: Why is it good to notice your own early signs of anger? | The earlier you catch it, the easier it is to calm down | It lets you get angry faster | It makes the anger last longer | It means you never feel angry about anything ever again
test: After an angry shout at dinner, what repairs things best? | Saying sorry once you are calm | Saying nothing and just waiting | Shouting again so that it is even | Leaving the house without a word
test: A girl in Brisbane thinks it is unfair that only her brother can stay up late. What should she do? | Explain calmly why she thinks it is unfair | Scream and cry until her parents finally give in | Stay up secretly under the blanket with a torch | Refuse to speak to anyone in the house for a day
test: Why is holding on to anger for a long time a bad idea? | It keeps you unhappy long after the problem | It makes the other person feel much sorrier | It keeps you safe from new problems | It shows everyone that you are strong and not to be messed with
test: so mad at my bro i wanna smack him | Walk away, breathe and count to ten | Smack him hard so he learns his lesson | Smack him softly, that is okay as long as it doesnt hurt | Throw his toys across the room instead
test: lost the level 10 times grr, one more go!! | Take a break and a drink, an angry brain plays worse | Keep going till you win | Smash the controller a bit | Play faster and press the buttons harder so you finally beat it
test: i broke his lego cos i was angry, now what | Tell an adult and think how to make it right | Hide it and say nothing | Say he broke it himself when he was playing with it | Break something of his too
test: gonna get her back tmrw for what she did | Revenge makes it worse, tell a teacher instead | Get her back twice as hard so she never does it again | Get her back, it is only fair | Plan it so no adult sees
explain: A boy in Ohio threw his tablet across the room when he lost a game; the screen smashed, and he had to save his pocket money for months for a new one.
explain: Two friends in Leeds shouted and shoved each other over a ball; one fell against a wall and cut his head, and both were very sorry afterwards.
fact: Acting in anger without stopping can break things, hurt people and end friendships.
fact: Taking slow breaths and stepping away for a few minutes helps the body calm down before you act.
test: lost the game AGAIN, gonna chuck my controller | Stop, step away and breathe, you might break it or hurt someone | Throw it, it lets anger out safely | Break something cheaper instead | Shout at the person who won
test: mate shoved me so i'm gonna shove him back harder | Step back and get an adult, pushing can hurt someone badly | Push back, it's fair | Hit him when no one sees | Push him really hard and then just say sorry later if a teacher asks
test: why count to 10 when ur mad, sounds dumb | It gives your body time to calm so you don't do something you regret | It makes the other person go away | Counting is only for maths | It makes you angrier n angrier the more numbers you count, so it's no use at all
test: did u hear Callum flipped a table in maths cos he lost at a quiz, chairs flew everywhere, they said he's getting expelled | Acting in anger without stopping breaks things and hurts people; the expelled part is likely just rumour | Flipping a table is fine if you really lost unfairly | It must be true because everyone is saying it | Anger always ends with chairs flying, it cannot be stopped
test: my brother reckons u should get revenge straight away or ppl think ur weak | No, revenge usually makes a fight bigger, so tell a teacher instead | Yes, quick payback stops people picking on you | Yes, but only if they hit you first | Yes, if you plan it carefully it works
test: Liam lost his temper n cried at footy, the lads are making a video of it to send round | Do not share it; everyone gets angry, and spreading it would be cruel | Share it, it is only a laugh | Share it, but blur his face a bit | Share it so he learns not to cry
exam: Which of the following measures is recommended when a person becomes aware of mounting anger? | Stopping, breathing slowly and counting to ten before speaking or acting | Responding at once, before the anger has an opportunity to diminish | Striking the nearest object in order to release the anger quickly | Shouting loudly so that others are aware of the anger immediately
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pause is beneficial at the onset of anger. | The thinking part of the brain catches up with the body only after a few seconds | The body ceases to produce any feeling whatsoever after precisely one minute | Anger can only be expressed in words once the person has fully forgotten it | A pause makes it more likely that the other person will apologise first
exam: Pursuant to the breakage of an object in a moment of anger, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The person informs an adult and considers how to make amends | The person conceals the broken object and denies all knowledge | The person attributes the breakage to a sibling or a classmate | The person breaks a further object so that the first is not noticed
exam: In respect of the expression of disagreement, which statement most accurately characterises the established facts? | People listen to a calm and clear reason far more readily than to a shout | People listen most attentively to the loudest voice present in the room | People listen to a reason only when it is accompanied by slammed doors | People listen to anger and to calmness to precisely the same degree
